    <div class="tab-section">
      <div class="ts-eyebrow">What makes Hamas structurally unusual</div>
      <div class="ts-title">A Sunni Organization in a Shia Axis — The Hamas Paradox</div>
      <div class="ts-body">Hamas is the most doctrinally anomalous member of the Axis of Resistance. Every other major Iranian proxy — Hezbollah, the Houthis, the PMF factions — is Shia. Hamas is Sunni. Its ideological lineage runs through the Egyptian Muslim Brotherhood, not the Islamic Revolution. Its founding charter drew on Sunni Islamist tradition. Yet it has received sustained Iranian funding, weapons, and training for decades.

The relationship works because both parties subordinate theology to strategy: Iran sees Hamas as a tool to project power to Israel's borders and destabilize the region; Hamas sees Iran as its most willing weapons supplier. When Hamas backed the Sunni opposition during the Syrian civil war (2012–2017), Iran cut funding and boosted PIJ instead — demonstrating the relationship is transactional, not theological. Hamas reconciled with Iran in 2022 with Iranian mediation.

This structural ambiguity defines Hamas's position in every framework: it is simultaneously a resistance movement (in Palestinian, Arab, and Global South framing), a terrorist organization (in Western and Israeli framing), a governing authority (in its administrative reality in Gaza), and an Iranian proxy (in geopolitical analysis). FORA records all four framings without resolving between them — that resolution is a CivicIssue, not a data point.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="tab-section">
      <div class="ts-eyebrow">Organizational structure</div>
      <div class="ts-title">Two Organizations in One: Political Bureau and Military Wing</div>
      <div class="ts-body">Hamas operates as two functionally distinct organizations that share ideology, leadership, and funding but present differently to the world. The distinction matters legally: the EU until 2021 only designated the military wing (Izz ad-Din al-Qassam Brigades), leaving the political bureau undesignated and able to maintain diplomatic contacts in Europe.</div>

      <div class="info-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:16px;margin-top:16px;">
        <div class="info-card">
          <div class="ic-title">Political Bureau</div>
          <div class="ic-body">Based in Qatar (with Turkish presence). Manages diplomacy, ceasefire negotiations, fundraising, international relations. Khalil al-Hayya is current senior figure following Haniyeh's assassination in Tehran July 2024. Conducts negotiations — directly engaged in Oct 2025 Gaza ceasefire talks. The bureau is intact and functioning despite the military campaign.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <div class="ic-title">Izz ad-Din al-Qassam Brigades</div>
          <div class="ic-body">Military wing, named after a 1930s Palestinian resistance leader. Based in Gaza tunnel network. Mohammed Deif (commander) killed Aug 2024. Marwan Issa (deputy) killed Mar 2024. The Oct 7 attack was planned and executed by this wing over years of operational security. Severely degraded by 2026 but not destroyed — estimates of 5,000–10,000 remaining fighters in Gaza tunnels.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

